{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sv1wekGV9xEhJcRWiE97uq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd065-factor\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/factor: Prime Factorization\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Shell scripts and mathematical workflows use factor to decompose integers into their prime\n6\t  factors. The macOS BSD system has a factor command but it differs from GNU factor in output\n7\t  formatting (spacing after the colon) and input handling (stdin vs arguments). The Go\n8\t  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gfactor, installed by\n9\t  coreutils) in output format and exit codes, verified by differential testing via\n10\t  pkg/testutils.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Print the prime factorization of each integer argument.\n14\t  - G2: Read integers from stdin when no arguments are given.\n15\t  - G3: \"Match GNU factor output format exactly (NUMBER: FACTOR FACTOR ...).\"\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Core factorization behavior\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: \"For each integer argument, must print a line in the format 'NUMBER: FACTOR FACTOR ...' where factors are in ascending order with repetition for multiplicity (e.g., '12: 2 2 3').\"\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: \"For the number 1, must print '1:' with no factors.\"\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: Must handle prime numbers by printing the number itself as the sole factor.\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: Must process multiple arguments in order, printing one factorization line per argument.\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Stdin mode and input handling\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: When no arguments are given, must read integers from stdin, one per line, and factorize each.\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: Must accept integers up to at least 2^63-1 (the Go int64 maximum).\n37\t      - R2.3:\n38\t          text: Must skip blank lines in stdin mode without producing output or error.\n39\t      - R2.4:\n40\t          text: When a non-integer or negative input is encountered, must print an error to stderr and continue processing remaining inputs.\n41\t\n42\t  R3:\n43\t    title: Help, version, and output\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R3.1:\n46\t          text: When --help is the first argument, must print a usage message to stdout and exit 0.\n47\t      - R3.2:\n48\t          text: When --version is the first argument, must print version information to stdout and exit 0.\n49\t      - R3.3:\n50\t          text: Must write factorization output to stdout, one line per integer.\n51\t      - R3.4:\n52\t          text: Must write error messages to stderr without stopping processing of remaining inputs.\n53\t\n54\t  R4:\n55\t    title: Exit codes and differential testing\n56\t    items:\n57\t      - R4.1:\n58\t          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are valid and factorized successfully.\n59\t      - R4.2:\n60\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input is invalid (non-integer, negative, overflow).\n61\t      - R4.3:\n62\t          text: \"Differential tests must compare stdout and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\"\n63\t      - R4.4:\n64\t          text: \"Tests must cover: single number, multiple numbers, primes, composites, 1, large numbers, stdin mode, error on non-integer, and error on negative number.\"\n65\t\n66\tnon_goals:\n67\t  - cmd/factor does not implement arbitrary-precision factorization beyond int64 range.\n68\t  - cmd/factor does not implement advanced factorization algorithms (Pollard rho, quadratic sieve). Trial division suffices for the int64 range.\n69\t\n70\tacceptance_criteria:\n71\t  - id: AC1\n72\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/factor 12 prints '12: 2 2 3', matching gfactor.\"\n73\t    traces:\n74\t      - R1.1\n75\t      - R1.4\n76\t  - id: AC2\n77\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/factor 1 prints '1:', matching gfactor.\"\n78\t    traces:\n79\t      - R1.2\n80\t  - id: AC3\n81\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/factor 97 prints '97: 97', matching gfactor.\"\n82\t    traces:\n83\t      - R1.3\n84\t  - id: AC4\n85\t    criterion: \"echo '15' | go run ./cmd/factor prints '15: 3 5', matching gfactor stdin mode.\"\n86\t    traces:\n87\t      - R2.1\n88\t      - R2.4\n89\t  - id: AC5\n90\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gfactor passes for all cases listed in R4.4.\"\n91\t    traces:\n92\t      - R4.3\n93\t      - R4.4\n94\t  - id: AC6\n95\t    criterion: \"cmd/factor compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n96\t    traces:\n97\t      - R1.1\n98\t      - R1.2\n99\t      - R1.3\n100\t      - R1.4\n101\t      - R2.1\n102\t      - R2.2\n103\t      - R2.3\n104\t      - R2.4\n105\t      - R3.1\n106\t      - R3.2\n107\t      - R3.3\n108\t      - R3.4\n109\t      - R4.1\n110\t      - R4.2\n111\t      - R4.3\n112\t      - R4.4\n113\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8154b1e0-ed8f-4bba-876a-8feaa333f5c9","uuid":"abd7f3b8-95f8-4c15-85ee-c8c42c3db5f1","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:57:06.755Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5428/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd065-factor.yaml","content":"id: srd065-factor\ntitle: \"cmd/factor: Prime Factorization\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Shell scripts and mathematical workflows use factor to decompose integers into their prime\n  factors. The macOS BSD system has a factor command but it differs from GNU factor in output\n  formatting (spacing after the colon) and input handling (stdin vs arguments). The Go\n  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gfactor, installed by\n  coreutils) in output format and exit codes, verified by differential testing via\n  pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Print the prime factorization of each integer argument.\n  - G2: Read integers from stdin when no arguments are given.\n  - G3: \"Match GNU factor output format exactly (NUMBER: FACTOR FACTOR ...).\"\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Core factorization behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: \"For each integer argument, must print a line in the format 'NUMBER: FACTOR FACTOR ...' where factors are in ascending order with repetition for multiplicity (e.g., '12: 2 2 3').\"\n      - R1.2:\n          text: \"For the number 1, must print '1:' with no factors.\"\n      - R1.3:\n          text: Must handle prime numbers by printing the number itself as the sole factor.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must process multiple arguments in order, printing one factorization line per argument.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Stdin mode and input handling\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: When no arguments are given, must read integers from stdin, one per line, and factorize each.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must accept integers up to at least 2^63-1 (the Go int64 maximum).\n      - R2.3:\n          text: Must skip blank lines in stdin mode without producing output or error.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: When a non-integer or negative input is encountered, must print an error to stderr and continue processing remaining inputs.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Help, version, and output\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: When --help is the first argument, must print a usage message to stdout and exit 0.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: When --version is the first argument, must print version information to stdout and exit 0.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: Must write factorization output to stdout, one line per integer.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: Must write error messages to stderr without stopping processing of remaining inputs.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are valid and factorized successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input is invalid (non-integer, negative, overflow).\n      - R4.3:\n          text: \"Differential tests must compare stdout and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\"\n      - R4.4:\n          text: \"Tests must cover: single number, multiple numbers, primes, composites, 1, large numbers, stdin mode, error on non-integer, and error on negative number.\"\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/factor does not implement arbitrary-precision factorization beyond int64 range.\n  - cmd/factor does not implement advanced factorization algorithms (Pollard rho, quadratic sieve). Trial division suffices for the int64 range.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/factor 12 prints '12: 2 2 3', matching gfactor.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/factor 1 prints '1:', matching gfactor.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.2\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/factor 97 prints '97: 97', matching gfactor.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.3\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"echo '15' | go run ./cmd/factor prints '15: 3 5', matching gfactor stdin mode.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.4\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gfactor passes for all cases listed in R4.4.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/factor compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n","numLines":113,"startLine":1,"totalLines":113}}}
